From: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media, drm: h713: follow the source geometry instead of a constant

When the source changes its resolution the wall shows the new picture torn
across the top and the old one underneath (doku/nachtlog/A6-4). The firmware
is not the reason. It re-detects the timing, rewrites its capture and lays
the ring out for the new picture on its own: at 1280x720 INCAP +0x924 reads
0x50 = 80 = 1280/16 where it read 0x78 = 120 = 1920/16 before, +0x874 reads
0x050002d0 and +0x548 0x02ee0672 (doku/nachtlog/E4, register dumps of both
modes). The ring really holds 720 lines of 1280 bytes; the 480 rows of new
content in A6-4's reconstruction are exactly 921600 = 720 * 1280 bytes read
with a 1920 byte stride, and the "three copies side by side" are the 1.5
source lines that fit into one such row. Everything that reads the ring on
our side did so with a compile-time constant: H713_HDMIRX_WIDTH/HEIGHT at
three places in sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c (the format, the timings cap, the
answer to QUERY_DV_TIMINGS), a plane that refused any framebuffer other than
the panel size, and a userspace that built its framebuffer from the CRTC mode.
The firmware writes with 80, we read with 120; that is the tearing.

What the firmware tells us, and what it does not
------------------------------------------------

MipsHalCallback_SignalChange carries two words -- ParaCount 2 in the
firmware's own cpu_comm trace, "Para[] 0x00000003 0x4e332000" in our debugfs.
Para[0] is the source id, Para[1] the physical address of a 44 byte record in
the cpu_comm shared memory. The record has never been read; its layout is now
taken from display.bin rather than assumed:

  - The function that prints the elog lines "thal_display_source.cpp 204..214"
    (0x8b14b544) has the record at sp+0x20 and prints one word per line, in
    order, from +0x00 to +0x28: signal_id, frame_rate_x100, b_interlace,
    color_format, color_space, resolution.h_size, resolution.v_size, hdr_mode,
    b_full_range, b_dvi_mode, atv_uid. Eleven words, 44 bytes. source_id
    (line 203) comes from the function's first argument and is not in it.
  - The SignalChange adapter it then calls (0x8b109fb0) copies exactly 0x2c
    bytes of it into sgp_hal_signal_info, sets ParaCount 2, Para[0] = source
    id, and puts (ptr & 0x0fffffff | 0x80000000) + 0xc0000000 into Para[1]:
    0xae332000 becomes 0x4e332000, the address in our debugfs.
  - kHalSignalID_NONE is 0, _UNKNOWN 1, _NOCHANGE 2 (name table 0x8b14adc0);
    in the stock trace the "no signal" record also carries h_size 0.

So the record gives the active size, the frame rate to 1/100 Hz, the scan
type and the firmware's verdict. It does NOT give the totals or the sync
positions. Those appear in the firmware's log too (h_total 2200, v_total
1125, hde_start 192, vde_start 41) but from a different, 172 byte structure
that never leaves the MIPS. The totals are readable elsewhere: INCAP +0x548
holds v_total/h_total and +0x874 the active size, exact in both measured
modes (E4). The sync positions are readable nowhere on the ARM (E4 searched
the block for them; the record has none).

Two corrections fall out of the disassembly. The debugfs code read the
record's address from Para[2] and never showed it -- there is no third word.
doku/83 and doku/88 say "Para[2]" as well; that is the one-based count of
hdmi_seq.py's trace, not the zero-based args[] of the kernel API. And
doku/84's reading of "+16 = the descriptor's colour format plus one" does
not match the layout: +0x10 is color_space, and the value 1 seen there is
kHalColorSpace_BT709, which is what the firmware reports for this source.

What changes
------------

sun50i-h713-hdmirx:

  - struct h713_hdmirx_signal_info, the record as laid out above, with the
    static_assert on its size. The callback stores Para[1]; the record is
    read on demand (QUERY_DV_TIMINGS, debugfs) through a mapping made on
    first use, so the callback stays a spinlock and the reader always sees
    the newest state, which is what a client wants after a source change.

  - INCAP as a read-only syscon behind a new "allwinner,incap" phandle. Only
    two words are read, +0x874 and +0x548. Writing INCAP from the ARM wedges
    the capture until a cold start (doku/78 section 0, rule 6); a regmap
    that nobody writes is the form in which that rule needs no discipline.

  - h713_hdmirx_detect_timings(): frames must be arriving (flip pointers);
    the record must not say "none"; the record's active size must equal the
    block's, otherwise the change is still in flight and the answer is
    -ENOLCK; the pixel clock is h_total * v_total * frame rate; and a preset
    with the same active and total geometry and the clock within 1 % is
    taken whole, which is the step the firmware itself takes when it maps a
    timing onto its mode table ("detect new timing, signal ID=0x20000
    index=14"). Without a preset the blanking is reported as tc358743 reports
    it -- whole in hsync/vsync, porches 0 -- the convention
    v4l2_valid_dv_timings() spells out for receivers that cannot split it.
    Before the first callback of a boot the geometry comes from INCAP alone
    and the frame rate from the flip pointers, coarsely, and only a preset the
    two agree on is reported: the first QUERY of a boot may otherwise find
    no record, because the callbacks are armed by open() (0100) and the
    firmware pushes the record only on a change.

  - The format follows the timings, computed by v4l2_fill_pixfmt_mp() from
    NV16M and the active size, so bytesperline is the width -- which is the
    ring's pitch. QUERY_DV_TIMINGS makes what it found the current timing
    unless buffers are in flight; a streaming queue keeps its geometry and
    the client that got the source change event stops and asks again.

  - timings_cap opens to 640x480..1920x1080, 25.175..148.5 MHz, progressive,
    CEA-861/DMT plus custom. The upper bound is the panel and the largest
    source seen through the chain; the two sources above 1080 lines that
    were tried never reached the ring (E3). The lower bound is the smallest
    mode in the EDID this driver presents (VIC 1). Between the two only
    1280x720p60 has been captured; the range says what is accepted, not
    what has been measured. Interlaced is refused rather than described.

sun50i-h713-afbd:

  - In HDMI passthrough atomic_check accepts a framebuffer smaller than the
    panel, shown full screen: src the framebuffer, dst the CRTC, upscaling
    unbounded, downscaling refused (never seen to happen), pitch equal to the
    width (the ring's). The six geometry registers were always computed from
    the framebuffer; now the framebuffer may say 1280x720. The plane itself
    does not scale; the composition block behind it does, and the firmware
    programs it in the same UpdateWce that lays out the ring (doku/91 Punkt
    2, stock chain step 6). That the composition follows on our board is
    inferred from that chain and from INCAP following; it is not measured
    on the wall yet, and doku/91's acceptance for Punkt 2 is where it is.
    Memory mode keeps the old rule, panel size or nothing.

  - The VidDec descriptor is not rewritten for a new passthrough geometry
    and no longer warns about it. The record describes the VideoDec source
    and is read by the VideoDec detector alone; the firmware's own chain for
    an HDMI signal change has no descriptor in it (stock elog, doku/91). It
    stays as first published for the boot, which is the one thing the
    firmware is known to survive.

Device tree: syscon@6940000 for INCAP, the phandle on hdmi-rx, and the
binding comment. Kconfig: depends on MFD_SYSCON (=y in the build).

The userspace half -- hy310-tv builds its framebuffer from G_FMT and rebuilds
it on a SOURCE_CHANGE with the plane off in between, its counterpart of the
firmware's AV mute -- is hy310-tv.diff next to this patch.

What this patch does not establish, and where it gets decided
-------------------------------------------------------------

  - Whether MipsHalCallback_SignalChange fires on a pure geometry change
    without a source switch. A6-4 counted 0 before the callbacks were armed
    at all (0100 came later); the firmware-side re-detection is inferred
    from the ring contents. doku/91 Punkt 2.2 is the measurement.
  - Whether the composition block follows the source (0x05000174 scale,
    0x05000444/0x05000544 geometry, 0x05000844 pitch at 720p).
  - Whether the firmware's HDMI-side UpdateWce writes AFBD source-0
    registers -- in particular the chroma stride at 0x05600044, which this
    plane doubles for NV16 -- after the plane has programmed them.
  - Whether the first record of a boot arrives without a source switch; the
    INCAP-plus-flip-rate path covers that case, but it is a path, not a
    measurement.

Signed-off-by: well0nez <well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
---
diff -ruN a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi	2026-09-07 17:25:58.738846727 +0200
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/allwinner/sun50i-h713.dtsi	2026-09-07 17:40:13.949384856 +0200
@@ -1570,6 +1570,26 @@
 			status = "okay";
 		};
 
+		/*
+		 * INCAP, the MIPS firmware's HDMI capture block. Nothing on
+		 * the ARM drives it: the firmware programs it for every
+		 * signal it locks, including the line pitch of the capture
+		 * ring (doku/nachtlog/E4-geometrie-vollstaendig.md). But its
+		 * active and total geometry are the one place where the size
+		 * of what lands in the ring can be read, so the HDMI receiver
+		 * node looks at two of its words, +0x874 and +0x548.
+		 *
+		 * A syscon and not a device on purpose: there is no driver
+		 * for the block and there must be no writer -- writing INCAP
+		 * from the ARM wedges the capture until a cold start (doku/78
+		 * section 0, rule 6). It lives in the TVFE/TVCAP power
+		 * domain; while that is off the whole block reads zero.
+		 */
+		incap: syscon@6940000 {
+			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-incap", "syscon";
+			reg = <0x06940000 0x1000>;
+		};
+
 		/* R-domain pinctrl for PL/PM banks (IR, S_PWM, WLAN/BT GPIOs) */
 		r_pio: pinctrl@7022000 {
 			compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-r-pinctrl";
@@ -1900,7 +1920,8 @@
 	 * from the flip notifier of display@5600000 -- that node owns the
 	 * register pair, claims it, and reads it on every vsync anyway. All
 	 * three are dependencies in Kconfig, not resources here. What is left
-	 * to describe is the memory the capture lands in.
+	 * to describe is the memory the capture lands in, and the one block
+	 * the driver looks at without owning it.
 	 *
 	 * Binding:
 	 *   compatible     "allwinner,sun50i-h713-hdmirx"
@@ -1915,6 +1936,14 @@
 	 *                       rectangles, the Vp_Init staging area -- have to
 	 *                       live where the MIPS can address them, and this
 	 *                       is that place. Named, not hard coded.
+	 *   allwinner,incap  the syscon over the firmware's capture block
+	 *                    (INCAP, syscon@6940000 above). Read only, two
+	 *                    words: the active and the total size of what the
+	 *                    capture is writing, which is where the driver
+	 *                    gets the pixel clock and the blanking of the
+	 *                    timing it reports -- the firmware's own signal
+	 *                    record has the active size and the frame rate
+	 *                    but no totals (doku/nachtlog/E4).
 	 *
 	 * No interrupt: three independent searches of the firmware image found
 	 * the capture interrupt only inside the MIPS interrupt controller, and
@@ -1925,6 +1954,7 @@
 		compatible = "allwinner,sun50i-h713-hdmirx";
 		memory-region = <&mips_framebuf>, <&cpu_comm_reserved>;
 		memory-region-names = "hdmi-ring", "cpu-comm-shmem";
+		allwinner,incap = <&incap>;
 		status = "okay";
 	};
 
diff -ruN a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c	2026-09-07 17:25:58.737835219 +0200
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/tiny/sun50i-h713-afbd.c	2026-09-07 17:39:52.266610685 +0200
@@ -387,11 +387,24 @@
  * pointer went from nothing to the record. They are per-boot state, which is
  * also why the disable path leaves them standing.
  *
+ * In HDMI passthrough (@ring) the record is published once per boot with the
+ * geometry of the first enable and then left alone, whatever geometry later
+ * enables bring. That is not a refusal and does not warn: the record describes
+ * the *VideoDec* source and is read by the firmware's VideoDec detector alone
+ * (GetFrameInfo/ConvertFrameInfo2SignalInfo). The HDMI capture and the WCE
+ * behind it never look at it -- the firmware's own chain for an HDMI signal
+ * change, from "AV mute" to NotifySignalChange, has no descriptor in it at all
+ * (stock elog, doku/91 Punkt 2). The plane takes its geometry from the
+ * framebuffer, the firmware takes its geometry from the signal, and the record
+ * stands as first published for as long as the boot lasts -- the one thing the
+ * firmware is known to survive.
+ *
  * Returns true if the firmware has something new to look at here, i.e. if the
  * record or the pointer changed. See h713_afbd_wait_wce().
  */
 static bool h713_afbd_publish_video_info(struct h713_afbd *h, unsigned int w,
-					 unsigned int hgt, unsigned int stride)
+					 unsigned int hgt, unsigned int stride,
+					 bool ring)
 {
 	u32 rec[H713_VIDEO_INFO_WORDS];
 	u32 ptr = lower_32_bits(h->video_info_dma);
@@ -402,7 +415,7 @@
 	h713_afbd_build_video_info(rec, w, hgt, stride);
 	differs = memcmp(h->video_info, rec, sizeof(rec)) != 0;
 	write_record = differs && !h->video_info_published;
-	if (differs && h->video_info_published)
+	if (differs && h->video_info_published && !ring)
 		drm_warn(&h->drm,
 			 "refusing to rewrite the VidDec descriptor for %ux%u; the capture does not survive a second signal event\n",
 			 w, hgt);
@@ -747,6 +760,33 @@
 	h->video_active = false;
 }
 
+/*
+ * In HDMI passthrough the framebuffer describes the *source*: its size and
+ * stride are the ring's, and the ring holds whatever the firmware's capture
+ * writes -- 1920x1080 for a 1080p source, 720 lines of 1280 bytes for a 720p
+ * source (INCAP rowbyte 80 = 1280/16, doku/nachtlog/E4). Refusing a
+ * framebuffer that is not the panel size refuses the picture that is really
+ * there; that is what this check did until 07.09.2026, and it is why a 720p
+ * source stood torn on the wall (A6-4): the plane kept reading a 1280-byte
+ * ring with a 1920-byte stride.
+ *
+ * The plane itself does not scale. What scales is the composition block
+ * behind it (0x05000000, doku/89), and that block is the firmware's: it
+ * programs it for the signal it detected, in the same UpdateWce that lays the
+ * ring out (doku/91 Punkt 2, step 6 of the stock chain). So in passthrough a
+ * source smaller than the panel is accepted and, as far as KMS is concerned,
+ * shown full screen -- src is the whole framebuffer, dst the whole CRTC -- and
+ * the plane programs source 0 for the framebuffer's size and nothing else.
+ * That the composition really follows is inferred from the stock chain and
+ * from INCAP following, not yet measured on the wall; the acceptance in
+ * doku/91 Punkt 2 is where that gets measured. A source larger than the panel
+ * is refused: scaling down has never been seen, and no source above 1080
+ * lines has ever reached the ring (E3).
+ *
+ * Without the ring the buffer is fetched from memory, and there the old rule
+ * stands: the buffer has to be the panel size, because nothing behind the
+ * reader gets reprogrammed for it.
+ */
 static int h713_afbd_video_atomic_check(struct drm_plane *plane,
 					struct drm_atomic_state *state)
 {
@@ -756,18 +796,25 @@
 	struct drm_crtc_state *crtc_state;
 	struct drm_framebuffer *fb;
 	dma_addr_t y, c;
+	bool ring;
 	int ret;
 
 	new_state = drm_atomic_get_new_plane_state(state, plane);
 	if (!new_state->crtc)
 		return 0;
+	ring = to_h713_plane_state(new_state)->hdmi_ring;
 
 	crtc_state = drm_atomic_get_crtc_state(state, new_state->crtc);
 	if (IS_ERR(crtc_state))
 		return PTR_ERR(crtc_state);
 
+	/*
+	 * A minimum scale of 1 means "any amount of upscaling": the bounds
+	 * that matter are the geometry checks below, which are about the
+	 * ring, not about a scaler whose limits nobody has measured.
+	 */
 	ret = drm_atomic_helper_check_plane_state(new_state, crtc_state,
-						  DRM_PLANE_NO_SCALING,
+						  ring ? 1 : DRM_PLANE_NO_SCALING,
 						  DRM_PLANE_NO_SCALING,
 						  false, false);
 	if (ret)
@@ -779,31 +826,40 @@
 	    (fb->format->format != DRM_FORMAT_NV12 &&
 	     fb->format->format != DRM_FORMAT_NV16) ||
 	    fb->modifier != DRM_FORMAT_MOD_LINEAR ||
-	    fb->width != mode->hdisplay ||
-	    fb->height != mode->vdisplay ||
+	    fb->width > mode->hdisplay ||
+	    fb->height > mode->vdisplay ||
 	    fb->pitches[1] != fb->pitches[0] ||
 	    fb->width % H713_VIDEO_BLOCK || fb->height % 2 ||
+	    fb->width < H713_VIDEO_BLOCK ||
 	    fb->height < H713_VIDEO_BLOCK ||
 	    new_state->src_x || new_state->src_y ||
-	    new_state->src_w != mode->hdisplay << 16 ||
-	    new_state->src_h != mode->vdisplay << 16 ||
+	    new_state->src_w != fb->width << 16 ||
+	    new_state->src_h != fb->height << 16 ||
 	    new_state->crtc_x || new_state->crtc_y ||
 	    new_state->crtc_w != mode->hdisplay ||
 	    new_state->crtc_h != mode->vdisplay)
 		return -EINVAL;
 
-	if (to_h713_plane_state(new_state)->hdmi_ring) {
+	if (ring) {
 		/*
 		 * In HDMI passthrough the framebuffer only describes format
 		 * and geometry; the addresses come from the capture ring, so
 		 * the ring has to be described in DT and the descriptor page
-		 * has to be somewhere the MIPS can read.
+		 * has to be somewhere the MIPS can read. And the stride has
+		 * to be the ring's: lines are exactly one width apart there
+		 * (INCAP rowbyte = width / 16), so a padded pitch would
+		 * program a stride for data this buffer does not own.
 		 */
 		if (!h->ring_size)
 			return -EOPNOTSUPP;
+		if (fb->pitches[0] != fb->width)
+			return -EINVAL;
 		return 0;
 	}
 
+	if (fb->width != mode->hdisplay || fb->height != mode->vdisplay)
+		return -EINVAL;
+
 	y = drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(fb, new_state, 0);
 	c = drm_fb_dma_get_gem_addr(fb, new_state, 1);
 	if (!y || !c || upper_32_bits(y) || upper_32_bits(c))
@@ -849,7 +905,7 @@
 		if (nv16)
 			writel(0, h->regs + AFBD_VIDEO_C_STRIDE);
 		published = h713_afbd_publish_video_info(h, w, hgt,
-							 fb->pitches[0]);
+							 fb->pitches[0], ring);
 		if (published && nv16 && h713_afbd_wait_wce(h))
 			drm_err(&h->drm,
 				"firmware did not reprogram the chroma stride within %u ms of the descriptor; source 0 is programmed anyway, so this enable may come up with a halved chroma stride\n",
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig	2026-09-07 17:40:13.942481713 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/Kconfig	2026-09-07 17:40:13.949474656 +0200
@@ -8,6 +8,7 @@
 	depends on HY310_CPU_COMM
 	depends on SUN50I_H713_ARISC
 	depends on DRM_SUN50I_H713_AFBD
+	depends on MFD_SYSCON
 	select VIDEOBUF2_CORE
 	select VIDEOBUF2_MEMOPS
 	help
diff -ruN a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c
--- a/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 17:25:58.736822692 +0200
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/sunxi/sun50i-h713-hdmirx/sun50i-h713-hdmirx.c	2026-09-07 17:44:56.784678773 +0200
@@ -35,7 +35,9 @@
  *  - "Signal present" is a measurement, not a register bit we can read: on
  *    signal loss the flip pointers stop moving and the last picture stays
  *    (measured 07.09.2026, doku/nachtlog/K4-hotplug.md). QUERY_DV_TIMINGS
- *    samples them for one frame period rather than trusting a cached flag.
+ *    watches them for a few frame periods rather than trusting a cached
+ *    flag, and takes the geometry from what the firmware reports and from
+ *    its capture block -- see h713_hdmirx_detect_timings().
  *
  *  - The firmware announces signal loss and signal return on its own through
  *    the MipsHalCallback_SignalChange callback (same measurement). The V4L2
@@ -50,16 +52,21 @@
  * appendix A (the seven stages of a source change).
  */
 
+#include <linux/build_bug.h>
 #include <linux/delay.h>
 #include <linux/debugfs.h>
 #include <linux/firmware.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
+#include <linux/ktime.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
+#include <linux/mfd/syscon.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/mod_devicetable.h>
 #include <linux/notifier.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/of_address.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/regmap.h>
 #include <linux/seq_file.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/spinlock.h>
@@ -85,18 +92,91 @@
 #define H713_HDMIRX_NAME	"sun50i-h713-hdmirx"
 
 /*
- * Geometry. The whole chain -- the firmware's window computation, the frame
- * descriptor the display driver publishes, the AFBD crop -- is set up for
- * 1920x1080p60 and nothing else has ever been measured on this board. A
- * resolution change is an open point (doku/78 appendix A.6 item 4), which is
- * why there is exactly one entry in the timings table below.
+ * Geometry. Nothing about the picture size is a constant of this driver: the
+ * firmware detects the source timing, reports it through the SignalChange
+ * callback (struct h713_hdmirx_signal_info below) and rewrites its capture
+ * for it -- for a 1280x720 source the ring holds 720 lines of 1280 bytes,
+ * measured 07.09.2026 (doku/nachtlog/E4-geometrie-vollstaendig.md, INCAP
+ * +0x924 = 80 = 1280/16). What is fixed is the pixel format the capture
+ * writes, NV16 in two planes, and the three slots it writes into.
+ *
+ * 1920x1080 is the panel. It is the window the bring-up sequence hands to
+ * Wce_SetWindow, and it is the initial timing -- what G_FMT answers before
+ * the first QUERY_DV_TIMINGS of a boot has replaced it. A default, not a
+ * measurement.
  */
 #define H713_HDMIRX_WIDTH	1920
 #define H713_HDMIRX_HEIGHT	1080
 
 /* NV16: chroma at full height, so both planes are width * height bytes. */
 #define H713_HDMIRX_PLANES	2
-#define H713_HDMIRX_PLANE_SIZE	(H713_HDMIRX_WIDTH * H713_HDMIRX_HEIGHT)
+
+/*
+ * The signal information record. MipsHalCallback_SignalChange carries two
+ * words: Para[0] is the source id (3 = HDMI-1), Para[1] the physical address
+ * of this record in the cpu_comm shared memory (ParaCount 2 in the firmware's
+ * own trace; "Para[] 0x00000003 0x4e332000" in debugfs).
+ *
+ * Layout from display.bin, not from the elog alone. The function that prints
+ * the elog lines "thal_display_source.cpp 204..214" (0x8b14b544) has the
+ * record at sp+0x20 and reads one word per line, in this order, at +0, +4,
+ * ... +0x28; source_id (line 203) is printed from its first argument and is
+ * not part of the record. The SignalChange adapter it then calls (0x8b109fb0,
+ * "_TSignalChangeCallback") copies exactly 0x2c bytes of it into
+ * sgp_hal_signal_info, sets ParaCount 2, Para[0] = source id, and puts the
+ * record's address converted to ARM physical -- (p & 0x0fffffff | 0x80000000)
+ * + 0xc0000000, 0xae332000 -> 0x4e332000 -- into Para[1]. Field names are the
+ * elog's; enumerator values as far as the name tables in the image give them.
+ *
+ * What is NOT in it: the total sizes and the sync positions. Those appear in
+ * the firmware's log too (h_total, v_total, hde_start, vde_start), but from a
+ * different, 172 byte structure that stays inside the MIPS. See
